# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-avc/openavc-drivers/main/avcdriver.schema.json
#
# Bose Professional ControlSpace EX-4ML / EX-8ML Dante endpoints
#
# Controls the EX-4ML (four mic/line inputs) and EX-8ML (eight) over the
# endpoint commands of Bose Professional's ControlSpace Serial Control
# Protocol v5.15 (revision date May 27, 2025): section 2, "Using
# Serial-over-Ethernet (WP, EP and EX Endpoints)", and section 8, "Endpoint
# Commands". ASCII text over UDP port 49494 (fixed), each command and each
# reply ending in a carriage return. Section 8 reads word for word as it did in
# v5.13 (June 2024); only the page numbers moved.
#
# Wire format (section 3, "MSA12X and Endpoint Commands"): capital letters,
# parameters separated by one space, numbers as plain text ("15.0"). The
# endpoint answers "ACK" and an echo of the command with the value now in
# force, or "NACK". A set's own answer therefore reports the new value, and
# each rule below reads a set's acknowledgement and a query's reply alike.
#
# What the endpoint reports. Per channel: input gain (IG c Q), phantom power
# (PP c Q) and the logic input (RLI p: one per channel, 1-8, so input p is
# channel p), plus a four-step signal level for every channel in one reply
# (RSL). The logic outputs (8.7: two per channel, 8 on an EX-4ML and 16 on an
# EX-8ML) are numbered 1-16 by the protocol (WLO p / RLO p). Each channel's
# connector labels its two outputs O1 and O2, and the document does not say
# which protocol number drives which pin, so each output is a child of its own
# under the protocol's number.
#
# Every read asks for one channel or one output. The document's "0 = all"
# forms (IG 0 Q, PP 0 Q, RLI 0, RLO 0) answer with several lines whose form it
# does not settle: its all-outputs example answers RLO 0 with "ACK RLI n s"
# lines, which would read as logic inputs, and its single-output example
# answers RLO 2 with "ACK WLO 2 1". The output rule reads either word.
#
# The model. RSL answers 8 values on an EX-8ML and 4 on an EX-4ML (8.6), and
# no other endpoint takes RSL. That count sets the Model reading and both
# rosters, which are saved into Channels and Logic Outputs (learned_from).
#
# Push. EVNT and SASIP (8.9, 8.10) make the endpoint send a UDP packet to one
# address and port when a logic input changes. The document does not give the
# packet's contents, and an endpoint paired with an EX processor uses those
# settings for the processor ("making changes to these settings could cause
# unexpected side-effects in that scenario"). So this driver sends neither: it
# reads every logic input on each poll, first in the poll.
#
# A paired endpoint. When the endpoint is part of a ControlSpace design, the EX
# processor's own settings can replace anything sent here (sections 2 and 8).
# The readings stay correct either way, since every value is read back.
#
# Not in the document, so not here: Bose's Crestron module for the EX-8ML also
# saves and loads presets 0-9, and section 8 has no preset command.
#
# Liveness: every poll asks questions the endpoint answers in every state, so
# from platform 0.37.0 three polls with no reply take it offline.
#
# Format: YAML. Every reply is one line naming its channel or output, and the
# one list reply (RSL) fills the channels by position.
#
# NOT hardware-verified. Built from the document and its own examples.

id: bose_controlspace_ex_ml
name: Bose ControlSpace EX-4ML / EX-8ML Endpoint
manufacturer: Bose Professional
category: audio
version: "1.0.0"
author: OpenAVC
min_platform_version: "0.37.0"
description: >
  Controls the Bose Professional ControlSpace EX-4ML and EX-8ML mic/line Dante
  endpoints over UDP (port 49494). For every channel: input gain (0, +15, +30
  or +45 dB), phantom power, the logic input and the signal level. Every logic
  output can be read and switched. Identify, Find Unit and factory defaults.
  The endpoint's model sets the channel and logic output counts.

source_url: https://assets.boseprofessional.com/m/4b14907043cbedb3/original/ControlSpace-Serial-Protocol-v5-15.zip
tags: [dante, endpoint, microphone, preamp, gpio, controlspace]
verified: false
simulated: true
protocols: [bose_controlspace_endpoint]
ports: [49494]

compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: Bose Professional
    models: [EX-4ML, EX-8ML]
    confidence: untested
    notes: >
      Built from the ControlSpace Serial Control Protocol v5.15; not yet run
      against an endpoint. When the endpoint is part of a ControlSpace design,
      the EX processor's settings can replace gain, phantom power and logic
      output changes sent here; make those changes in ControlSpace Designer.
      The EX processors themselves use the Bose ControlSpace driver.

transport: udp

help:
  overview: >
    Shows each channel of a Bose ControlSpace EX-4ML or EX-8ML endpoint: its
    input gain, phantom power, logic input and signal level, and the state of
    every logic output. Gain, phantom power and logic outputs can be changed
    here. Identify flashes the endpoint's power LED until it is turned off;
    Find Unit flashes it for 5 seconds.
  setup: >
    1. Give the endpoint a static IP address or a DHCP reservation (Dante
       Controller or ControlSpace Designer sets it), and note it.
    2. Enter that address here. Control uses UDP port 49494; leave the port
       alone.
    3. Choose Channels: 4 for an EX-4ML, 8 for an EX-8ML. Once connected,
       the endpoint's own report sets Channels and Logic Outputs and saves
       them here.
    4. If the endpoint is part of a ControlSpace design, the EX processor's
       settings can replace gain, phantom power and logic output changes
       made here. Make those changes in ControlSpace Designer, and use this
       driver to read them and to see the logic inputs.
    5. Logic inputs are read at the start of every poll, and a poll of an
       EX-8ML takes about 2 seconds, so a change shows within the Poll
       Interval plus 2 seconds. A press on a button wired to a logic input
       that is shorter than that may not show.
  connection: >
    The endpoint answers on UDP port 49494. If ControlSpace Designer or Dante
    Controller sees it and OpenAVC does not, a firewall between the server and
    the endpoint is blocking UDP 49494.

discovery:
  # RSL (8.6) is answered by the EX-4ML and EX-8ML only, with one level per
  # channel. The MSA12X and the other endpoints on this port have no RSL, so
  # the probe names these two models and no other Bose device.
  udp_probe:
    port: 49494
    send_ascii: "RSL\r"
    expect_regex: '^ACK RSL(?: -?\d+){4}(?:(?: -?\d+){4})?\s*$'
    timeout_ms: 1500
    extract_manufacturer: "Bose Professional"
  manufacturer_alias:
    - "bose"
    - "bose professional"

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 49494
  channel_count: 8
  logic_output_count: 16
  poll_interval: 3
  # The document states no minimum gap between messages. A poll of an EX-8ML
  # is 42 messages, which take 2.1 s at this spacing.
  inter_command_delay: 0.05

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 49494
    label: UDP Port
    help: "The endpoint listens on UDP port 49494."
  channel_count:
    type: enum
    values: [4, 8]
    default: 8
    label: Channels
    learned_from: channels_reported
    help: >
      4 for an EX-4ML, 8 for an EX-8ML. Once connected, the endpoint's own
      report sets it, and it is saved here.
  logic_output_count:
    type: enum
    values: [8, 16]
    default: 16
    label: Logic Outputs
    learned_from: logic_outputs_reported
    help: >
      8 for an EX-4ML, 16 for an EX-8ML (two per channel). Once connected,
      the endpoint's own report sets it, and it is saved here.
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 3
    min: 3
    max: 60
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    help: >
      The wait between polls. Every reading is refreshed on each poll, logic
      inputs first, and a poll of an EX-8ML takes about 2 seconds, so a logic
      input change shows within this interval plus 2 seconds.
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.05
    min: 0
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    help: "Pause between messages sent to the endpoint."

state_variables:
  model:
    type: string
    label: Model
    help: "EX-4ML or EX-8ML, from the number of channels the endpoint reports."
  channels_reported:
    type: integer
    label: Channels
    help: "The number of mic/line channels the endpoint reports."
  logic_outputs_reported:
    type: integer
    label: Logic Outputs
    help: "The number of logic outputs: two per channel."
  firmware_version:
    type: string
    label: Firmware Version
  identify:
    type: boolean
    label: Identify
    help: "The endpoint's power LED is flashing so it can be found. It stays on until turned off."

child_entity_types:
  channel:
    label: Channel
    label_plural: Channels
    id_format: { type: integer, min: 1, max: 8 }
    state_variables:
      gain:
        type: number
        label: Input Gain
        min: 0
        max: 45
        step: 15
        unit: dB
        control: true
        help: "The channel's preamp gain: 0, +15, +30 or +45 dB."
      phantom_power:
        type: boolean
        label: Phantom Power
        control: true
        help: "48 V phantom power on this input."
      logic_input:
        type: boolean
        label: Logic Input
        cloud_priority: high
        help: "The channel's logic input (I1 on its connector) is on, as the endpoint reads it after debouncing."
      signal_level:
        type: integer
        label: Signal Level
        min: -99
        max: -3
        unit: dB
        cloud_priority: low
        help: "-99: no signal (below -40 dB). -40: at least -40 dB. -9: at least -9 dB. -3: at least -3 dB."
      signal_present:
        type: boolean
        label: Signal Present
        cloud_priority: low
        help: "The input's level is at -40 dB or above."
    summary_fields: [gain, phantom_power, logic_input, signal_present]
    instances:
      count_from_state: channels_reported
      count_from: channel_count
      label: "Channel {id}"
  logic_output:
    label: Logic Output
    label_plural: Logic Outputs
    id_format: { type: integer, min: 1, max: 16 }
    state_variables:
      active:
        type: boolean
        label: "On"
        control: true
        help: "The logic output is on."
    summary_fields: [active]
    instances:
      count_from_state: logic_outputs_reported
      count_from: logic_output_count
      label: "Logic Output {id}"

commands:
  set_input_gain:
    label: Set Input Gain
    help: "Sets the channel's preamp gain to 0, +15, +30 or +45 dB."
    send: "IG {channel} {gain}\r"
    sets: { gain: "{gain}" }
    params:
      channel:
        type: child_id
        child_type: channel
        required: true
        label: Channel
      gain:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Gain
        # An enum passes a value it does not list through to the device; the
        # pattern refuses one before it is sent (labels resolve first).
        pattern: '0\.0|15\.0|30\.0|45\.0'
        values:
          - { value: "0.0", label: "0 dB" }
          - { value: "15.0", label: "+15 dB" }
          - { value: "30.0", label: "+30 dB" }
          - { value: "45.0", label: "+45 dB" }

  set_phantom_power:
    label: Set Phantom Power
    help: "Turns 48 V phantom power on or off for the channel."
    send: "PP {channel} {enabled}\r"
    sets: { phantom_power: "{enabled}" }
    params:
      channel:
        type: child_id
        child_type: channel
        required: true
        label: Channel
      enabled:
        type: boolean
        required: true
        label: Phantom Power
        map: { "true": "1", "false": "0" }

  set_logic_output:
    label: Set Logic Output
    help: "Turns the logic output on or off."
    send: "WLO {output} {enabled}\r"
    sets: { active: "{enabled}" }
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: logic_output
        required: true
        label: Logic Output
      enabled:
        type: boolean
        required: true
        label: "On"
        map: { "true": "1", "false": "0" }

  set_identify:
    label: Identify
    help: "Flashes the endpoint's power LED until it is turned off, so the endpoint can be found."
    send: "ID {enabled}\r"
    sets: { identify: "{enabled}" }
    params:
      enabled:
        type: boolean
        required: true
        label: Flashing
        map: { "true": "1", "false": "0" }

  find_unit:
    label: Find Unit
    help: "Flashes the endpoint's LEDs for 5 seconds."
    send: "FU\r"

  restore_defaults:
    label: Restore Factory Defaults
    help: >
      Sets every input gain to 0 dB and turns phantom power and every logic
      output off. These become the settings the endpoint starts with.
    confirm: >
      Sets every input gain to 0 dB and turns phantom power and every logic
      output off, and makes these the settings the endpoint starts with.
    send: "DEFAULTS\r"

quick_actions: [set_logic_output, set_phantom_power, find_unit]

# The first lines size the rosters (RSL) and read what a poll does not need
# every few seconds (the firmware version). The poll repeats RSL and QUERY at
# once, so those two are not sent twice at connect.
on_connect:
  - { send: "VERSION\r", query_for: firmware_version }
  - { send: "RSL\r", query_for: channel.signal_level }
  - { send: "QUERY\r", query_for: identify }

# Logic inputs first, so a change shows as soon as the poll starts. A poll of
# an EX-8ML is 42 messages: 8 logic inputs, RSL, QUERY, 8 gains, 8 phantom
# power states and 16 logic outputs.
polling:
  queries:
    - { each_child: channel, send: "RLI {child_id}\r", query_for: logic_input }
    - { send: "RSL\r", query_for: channel.signal_level }
    - { send: "QUERY\r", query_for: identify }
    - { each_child: channel, send: "IG {child_id} Q\r", query_for: gain }
    - { each_child: channel, send: "PP {child_id} Q\r", query_for: phantom_power }
    - { each_child: logic_output, send: "RLO {child_id}\r", query_for: active }

responses:
  # 8.2: "ACK IG c g" answers a set and a query alike.
  - match: '^ACK IG (\d+) (\d+(?:\.\d+)?)$'
    child_set:
      - { type: channel, id: $1, state: { gain: $2 } }

  # 8.4: "ACK PP c s", 1 = on.
  - match: '^ACK PP (\d+) ([01])$'
    child_set:
      - type: channel
        id: $1
        state:
          phantom_power: { group: 2, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }

  # 8.8: "ACK RLI p s". The 0 that opens an all-inputs answer names no
  # channel and is skipped.
  - match: '^ACK RLI (\d+) ([01])$'
    child_set:
      - type: channel
        id: $1
        state:
          logic_input: { group: 2, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }

  # 8.7: "ACK WLO p s" answers a set; a query is answered "ACK RLO p s" in the
  # syntax and "ACK WLO p s" in the example, so either word is read.
  - match: '^ACK (?:RLO|WLO) (\d+) ([01])$'
    child_set:
      - type: logic_output
        id: $1
        state:
          active: { group: 2, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }

  # 8.6: "ACK RSL" and one level per channel, in channel order. Eight values
  # are an EX-8ML, four an EX-4ML.
  - match: '^ACK RSL ((?:-?\d+ ){7}-?\d+)$'
    set:
      model: "EX-8ML"
      channels_reported: 8
      logic_outputs_reported: 16
    child_list:
      type: channel
      element: '(-?\d+)'
      separator: ' '
      state:
        signal_level: $1
        signal_present: { group: 1, map: { "-99": "false", "-40": "true", "-9": "true", "-3": "true" } }
  - match: '^ACK RSL ((?:-?\d+ ){3}-?\d+)$'
    set:
      model: "EX-4ML"
      channels_reported: 4
      logic_outputs_reported: 8
    child_list:
      type: channel
      element: '(-?\d+)'
      separator: ' '
      state:
        signal_level: $1
        signal_present: { group: 1, map: { "-99": "false", "-40": "true", "-9": "true", "-3": "true" } }

  # 8.1: "ACK ID s".
  - match: '^ACK ID ([01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: identify, map: { "1": "true", "0": "false" } }

  # 8.11: the snapshot ends "ID=ON" or "ID=OFF". Gains and phantom power are
  # read per channel instead: the snapshot's fields differ by model.
  - match: '^ACK QUERY .*\bID=(ON|OFF)\b'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: identify, map: { "ON": "true", "OFF": "false" } }

  # 8.13: "ACK VERSION x.x".
  - match: '^ACK VERSION (\S+)$'
    set:
      firmware_version: $1

  # 8.1 and 8.12 acknowledge with nothing to read. A NACK is left unread.
  - match: '^ACK (?:FU|DEFAULTS)$'
    set: {}

simulator:
  # A model's channels come from the Channels setting (4 or 8); logic outputs
  # are two per channel. Signal levels and logic inputs are set here or in the
  # Simulator UI.
  initial_state:
    identify: false
    firmware_version: "2.020"
    channel.1.logic_input: false
    channel.1.signal_level: "-40"
    channel.2.logic_input: false
    channel.2.signal_level: "-9"
    channel.3.logic_input: false
    channel.3.signal_level: "-99"
    channel.4.logic_input: false
    channel.4.signal_level: "-99"
    channel.5.logic_input: false
    channel.5.signal_level: "-99"
    channel.6.logic_input: false
    channel.6.signal_level: "-99"
    channel.7.logic_input: false
    channel.7.signal_level: "-99"
    channel.8.logic_input: false
    channel.8.signal_level: "-99"
  controls:
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.1.logic_input, label: Channel 1 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.1.signal_level, label: Channel 1 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.2.logic_input, label: Channel 2 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.2.signal_level, label: Channel 2 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.3.logic_input, label: Channel 3 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.3.signal_level, label: Channel 3 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.4.logic_input, label: Channel 4 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.4.signal_level, label: Channel 4 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.5.logic_input, label: Channel 5 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.5.signal_level, label: Channel 5 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.6.logic_input, label: Channel 6 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.6.signal_level, label: Channel 6 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.7.logic_input, label: Channel 7 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.7.signal_level, label: Channel 7 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
    - { type: toggle, key: channel.8.logic_input, label: Channel 8 Logic Input }
    - { type: select, key: channel.8.signal_level, label: Channel 8 Signal Level (dB), options: ["-99", "-40", "-9", "-3"] }
  # One dispatcher owns the whole grammar: the answers depend on the model
  # (Channels) and a "Q" turns a set into a query. Fidelity gaps, all
  # undocumented behaviour: a value outside the document's list is refused
  # NACK (the document does not say whether the endpoint rounds it); the
  # "0 = all" reads are refused NACK, since their answers are several lines
  # and this driver never sends them; EVNT and SASIP are acknowledged and send
  # nothing.
  command_handlers:
    - match: '(?s:.+)'
      handler: |
        _send = respond

        def _run():
            line = match.group(0).strip()
            if not line:
                return
            try:
                chans = int(str(config.get("channel_count", 8)).strip())
            except ValueError:
                chans = 8
            if chans not in (4, 8):
                chans = 8
            outs = chans * 2
            parts = line.split()
            cmd = parts[0]
            args = parts[1:]

            def on(v):
                return str(v).strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes", "on")

            def num(text, lo, hi):
                try:
                    n = int(text)
                except ValueError:
                    return None
                return n if lo <= n <= hi else None

            def gain_text(c):
                try:
                    return "%.1f" % float(state.get("channel.%d.gain" % c) or 0)
                except (TypeError, ValueError):
                    return "0.0"

            def level(c):
                v = num(str(state.get("channel.%d.signal_level" % c) or "-99").strip(), -99, 0)
                return v if v in (-99, -40, -9, -3) else -99

            def nack():
                _send("NACK\r")

            if cmd == "FU" and not args:
                _send("ACK FU\r")
                return
            if cmd == "ID" and len(args) == 1 and args[0] in ("0", "1"):
                state["identify"] = args[0] == "1"
                _send("ACK ID %s\r" % args[0])
                return
            if cmd == "IG" and len(args) == 2:
                c = num(args[0], 1, chans)
                if c is None:
                    nack()
                    return
                if args[1] == "Q":
                    _send("ACK IG %d %s\r" % (c, gain_text(c)))
                    return
                try:
                    g = float(args[1])
                except ValueError:
                    nack()
                    return
                if g not in (0.0, 15.0, 30.0, 45.0):
                    nack()
                    return
                state["channel.%d.gain" % c] = g
                _send("ACK IG %d %.1f\r" % (c, g))
                return
            if cmd == "PP" and len(args) == 2:
                c = num(args[0], 1, chans)
                if c is None:
                    nack()
                    return
                if args[1] == "Q":
                    _send("ACK PP %d %d\r" % (c, int(on(state.get("channel.%d.phantom_power" % c)))))
                    return
                if args[1] not in ("0", "1"):
                    nack()
                    return
                state["channel.%d.phantom_power" % c] = args[1] == "1"
                _send("ACK PP %d %s\r" % (c, args[1]))
                return
            if cmd == "RLI" and len(args) == 1:
                c = num(args[0], 1, chans)
                if c is None:
                    nack()
                    return
                _send("ACK RLI %d %d\r" % (c, int(on(state.get("channel.%d.logic_input" % c)))))
                return
            if cmd == "WLO" and len(args) == 2:
                p = num(args[0], 1, outs)
                if p is None or args[1] not in ("0", "1"):
                    nack()
                    return
                state["logic_output.%d.active" % p] = args[1] == "1"
                _send("ACK WLO %d %s\r" % (p, args[1]))
                return
            if cmd == "RLO" and len(args) == 1:
                p = num(args[0], 1, outs)
                if p is None:
                    nack()
                    return
                _send("ACK RLO %d %d\r" % (p, int(on(state.get("logic_output.%d.active" % p)))))
                return
            if cmd == "RSL" and not args:
                _send("ACK RSL %s\r" % " ".join(str(level(c)) for c in range(1, chans + 1)))
                return
            if cmd == "QUERY" and not args:
                fields = ["IG%d=%s" % (c, gain_text(c)) for c in range(1, chans + 1)]
                fields += ["PP%d=%s" % (c, "ON" if on(state.get("channel.%d.phantom_power" % c)) else "OFF")
                           for c in range(1, chans + 1)]
                fields.append("ID=%s" % ("ON" if on(state.get("identify")) else "OFF"))
                _send("ACK QUERY %s \r" % " ".join(fields))
                return
            if cmd == "VERSION" and not args:
                _send("ACK VERSION %s\r" % (state.get("firmware_version") or "2.020"))
                return
            if cmd == "DEFAULTS" and not args:
                for c in range(1, chans + 1):
                    state["channel.%d.gain" % c] = 0.0
                    state["channel.%d.phantom_power" % c] = False
                for p in range(1, outs + 1):
                    state["logic_output.%d.active" % p] = False
                _send("ACK DEFAULTS\r")
                return
            if cmd in ("EVNT", "SASIP") and args:
                _send("ACK %s\r" % line)
                return
            nack()

        _run()
